props.put(ProducerConfig.COMPRESSION_TYPE_CONFIG, "zstd")
No limit changes needed for this statement
```

### How does the claim-check pattern work?

```java
package com.bank.claimcheck;

public class ClaimCheckFlow {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Step 1: Upload PDF to S3");
        System.out.println("  s3://bank-statements/SAV2001/june.pdf (4.5 MB)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Step 2: Publish reference on Kafka");
        System.out.println("  {accountId: SAV2001, s3Key: .../june.pdf} (~100 bytes)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Step 3: Consumer reads reference, downloads from S3");
        System.out.println("  Kafka stays fast, no limit changes needed");
    }
}
```

```text
Step 1: Upload PDF to S3
  s3://bank-statements/SAV2001/june.pdf (4.5 MB)

Step 2: Publish reference on Kafka
  {accountId: SAV2001, s3Key: .../june.pdf} (~100 bytes)

Step 3: Consumer reads reference, downloads from S3
  Kafka stays fast, no limit changes needed
```

### What are the three options compared?

| Option | When | Pro | Con |
|---|---|---|---|
| Raise all limits | Occasional slightly-over-1MB | Simple config change | Memory/latency impact on cluster |
| Compression | Large compressible data | No config change if under limit | CPU cost (minimal for lz4/zstd) |
| Claim-check | Genuinely large blobs | Kafka stays fast | Extra storage + round trip |

### What are common mistakes?

```java
package com.bank.mistake;

public class SizeMistakes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("WRONG: raise only producer limit");
        System.out.println("  Broker still rejects");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("WRONG: raise producer + broker, forget consumer");
        System.out.println("  Record stored but consumer stalls");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("WRONG: raise producer + broker + consumer, forget replica fetcher");
        System.out.println("  Followers cannot replicate large batches");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("CORRECT: raise all four: producer, topic, consumer, replica fetcher");
    }
}
```

```text
WRONG: raise only producer limit
  Broker still rejects

WRONG: raise producer + broker, forget consumer
  Record stored but consumer stalls

WRONG: raise producer + broker + consumer, forget replica fetcher
  Followers cannot replicate large batches

CORRECT: raise all four: producer, topic, consumer, replica fetcher
```

### How do you detect and monitor oversized messages?

Detecting oversized messages before they cause exceptions is better than reacting after the fact. Use producer interceptors to measure record sizes, and alert when records approach the limit. On the broker side, monitor the `kafka.server:type=BrokerTopicMetrics,name=FailedProduceRequestsPerSec` metric.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

public class OversizedMessageMonitoring {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Monitoring oversized messages:");
        System.out.println("  1. Producer interceptor: log warning if size > 80% of limit");
        System.out.println("  2. Broker metric: FailedProduceRequestsPerSec");
        System.out.println("     Spike = producers sending oversized records");
        System.out.println("  3. Application logging: catch RecordTooLargeException");
        System.out.println("     Log the key, topic, and actual size for debugging");
        System.out.println("  4. Pre-send validation:");
        System.out.println("     if (serializedSize > MAX_RECORD_SIZE * 0.8)");
        System.out.println("       use claim-check pattern instead");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Monitoring oversized messages:
  1. Producer interceptor: log warning if size > 80% of limit
  2. Broker metric: FailedProduceRequestsPerSec
     Spike = producers sending oversized records
  3. Application logging: catch RecordTooLargeException
     Log the key, topic, and actual size for debugging
  4. Pre-send validation:
     if (serializedSize > MAX_RECORD_SIZE * 0.8)
       use claim-check pattern instead
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Raising limits is a last resort because Kafka is optimized for many small records. Compression is the right first lever. The claim-check pattern keeps Kafka messages small. Any limit change must be rolled out across all layers and load-tested.
